"Acceptance Is Not Speed: Length Inflation in Training-Free Lossy Speculative Decoding (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23818v1)\nciteturn41240view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23818v1\",\"lineno\":153}); Total lines: 755\nL143: ### 2.3 Training-free methods\nL144: \nL145: Table cite79†A1 states every method as a choice of $(\\pi^{\\mathrm{rej}},\\pi^{\\mathrm{res}},\\pi^{\\mathrm{bonus}})$. The five swept rules fall into two groups: divergence-budgeted rules that boost the drafted token while bounding the distance from $p$ (CACTUS, mentored decoding), and switch rules that trust the drafter outright when a per-position test passes (fuzzy speculative decoding, speculative cascades).\nL146: The cascade token rule (spec_casc_tok) is not strict verification even at $\\alpha=0$: draft tokens inside a trusted top set of $p$ are accepted outright, and tokens outside it are judged against a scaled-down copy of $p$, so it is the only rule in the sweep that can reject what lossless verification would accept.\nL147: ## 3 Experimental Evaluation\nL148: \nL149: We measure what relaxed verification does to the generated text: five training-free rules, mentored_dec, cactus, spec_casc_opt, r_fuzzy, and spec_casc_tok, run on six datasets with twelve target-drafter pairs, each compared with lossless (strict) speculative decoding on the same pair. One (pair, dataset, rule) combination is a cell, and there are 360. For every cell we report completion length, verifier rounds (target forward passes), measured time, and task accuracy.\nL150: ### 3.1 Setup\nL151: #### Targets and drafters.\nL152: We use four targets in twelve target-drafter pairs (Table cite80†3 ): GPT-OSS-20B (cite81†OpenAI, 2025 ) with two EAGLE-3 heads (cite63†Li et al., 2025 ); Qwen3-8B (cite82†Yang et al., 2025 ) with an EAGLE-3 head, P-EAGLE (cite83†Hui et al., 2026 ), DSpark (cite84†Cheng et al., 2026 ), and the standalone Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen3-1.7B; Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (cite85†Grattafiori et al., 2024 ) with EAGLE-3, EAGLE-1 (cite61†Li et al., 2024b ), and the standalone Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct; and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (cite86†DeepSeek-AI, 2025 ) with EAGLE-3 and Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct (checkpoints in Appendix cite50†D ).\nL153: The EAGLE-1, EAGLE-3, P-EAGLE, and DSpark heads are the eight dedicated drafters; Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen3-1.7B, and Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct are the three standalone ones. Two pairs, GPT-OSS-20B with the nebius EAGLE-3 head and Qwen3-8B with the RedHatAI one,^{2}^{2} 2 nebius/EAGLE3-gpt-oss-20b and RedHatAI/Qwen3-8B-speculator.eagle3 on the Hugging Face Hub.\nL154: are measured in more depth (more seeds, timing on one server, run-log analyses, and the controls of Appendices cite39†B , cite43†C , and cite51†E ); we call them the primary pairs. Every pair runs on vLLM 0.26.0 on a single NVIDIA H100 with $N_{\\mathrm{draft}}=6$ (within $10\\%$ of the fastest lossless draft length; Appendix cite43†C ), sampled drafts, temperature $1.0$, top-$p$ $1.0$, and seed $0$; the bundled sampling defaults of Qwen3-8B and the Llama models are overridden (Appendices cite43†C and cite50†D ).\nL155: #### Implementation.\nL156: vLLM exposes no relaxed acceptance rule, so each method is a patch to its rejection sampler that implements the formulas of Table cite79†A1 , applied one at a time.\nL157: Each patch ships with tests that check the formula against closed-form values.^{3}^{3} 3 The EAGLE-3 head of Qwen3-8B, the EAGLE heads of the two Llama-based targets, and DSpark run through a second sampler path in vLLM, on which cactus and spec_casc_tok modify the acceptance test only and the residual distribution is the unmodified target’s; the other pairs apply the full rules.\nL158: Code, patches, prompts, and per-run outputs are released.^{4}^{4} 4 Anonymized repository: cite87†https://anonymous.4open.science/r/lossy-token-eff†anonymous.4open.science .\nL159: #### Datasets.\nL160: Six datasets cover four regimes: mathematics (GSM8K (cite88†Cobbe et al., 2021 ), AIME24 (cite89†Mathematical Association of America, 2024 )), code generation (HumanEval (cite90†Chen et al., 2021 ), LiveCodeBench (cite91†Jain et al., 2025 )), open-ended chat (MT-Bench (cite92†Zheng et al., 2023 )), and long-context multiple-choice question answering (LongBench-v2 (cite93†Bai et al., 2025 )).\nL161: GSM8K, AIME24, and LongBench-v2 are graded by exact match on the final answer; HumanEval and LiveCodeBench by executing the generated code against the problem’s tests. A run that never produces an answer is scored wrong. MT-Bench is scored by an LLM judge (Table cite94†B4 ). Case counts, generation budgets, and lossless references are listed in Tables cite95†B1 and cite96†D3 . A run that ends at its budget is counted as cut off.\nL162: #### Metrics.\nL163: We report five quantities: $\\bar{\\ell}$, the mean accepted length (draft tokens accepted per round); $\\bar{L}$, the mean completion length in tokens; $R$, the mean number of verifier rounds; the mean per-request wall-clock time, measured at the client at batch size 1 and excluding server start; and accuracy, the pass rate over the dataset’s cases (for MT-Bench, the mean judge score). Each case is run once per arm.\nL164: Every ratio to lossless is computed on the same cases for both arms, and its uncertainty is a 95% paired bootstrap interval over cases (10 000 resamples). A cell on a graded dataset (all but MT-Bench) loses accuracy when it falls two or more points below its lossless reference.\nL165: ### 3.2 Matching on mean accepted length\nL166: Because $\\alpha$ is not comparable across methods (Section cite9†2.2 ), we compare methods at matched $\\bar{\\ell}$. A short pilot runs each method’s four $\\alpha$ values (Table cite79†A1 ) on three cases per dataset; we then fix three target values of $\\bar{\\ell}$ and run the full dataset at the $\\alpha$ closest to each. The five methods never share a reachable $\\bar{\\ell}$, so the match is approximate. The four pairs with a standalone drafter and the P-EAGLE pair run each rule at its loosest $\\alpha$ only.\nL167: The tables report each method at its most relaxed setting; Sections cite21†3.3 and cite24†3.6 check that the conclusions do not depend on this choice.\nL168: ### 3.3 Completion length\nL169: \nL170: Relaxation lengthens completions in 248 of the 360 cells (Tables cite97†1 and cite80†3 ): $\\lambda$ has median $1.08$ and mean $1.54$, with the 95% interval above $1$ in 172 cells and below $1$ in 67. Its median varies more with the rule ($0.97$ for spec_casc_tok to $1.39$ for cactus) and the drafter ($0.94$ for standalone drafters to $1.23$ for EAGLE heads) than with the task ($1.04$ on GSM8K to $1.14$ on HumanEval), and cut-off runs make it a lower bound (Section cite25†3.7 ).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIgnored, Not Refused: What Low Prompt-Injection Success Rates Hidein Small Tool-Using Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23254v1)\nciteturn41240view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23254v1\",\"lineno\":290}); Total lines: 558\nL264:  | AgentDojo  | InjecAgent\nL265: --- | --- | ---\nL266: Agent  | Rec.  | Exec.$|$Rec.  | Rec.  | Exec.$|$Rec.\nL267: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL268: Gemma-4B  | 40.3  | 43.1  | 36.5  | 18.1\nL269: Gemma-12B  | 13.9  | 67.5  | 55.6  | 3.7\nL270: LFM-2.6B  | 23.6  | 13.8  | 10.4  | 1.9\nL271: MiniCPM-2B  | 12.0  | 25.0  | 6.1  | 9.8\nL272: Ministral-14B  | 8.8  | 82.6  | 26.7  | 26.7\nL273: Nanbeige-3B  | 16.1  | 26.1  | 56.9  | 3.3\nL274: Nemotron-Nano-4B  | 23.2  | 60.1  | 5.9  | 29.5\nL275: Ornith-9B  | 84.7  | 2.0  | 78.9  | 22.0\nL276: Qwen-4B  | 29.5  | 71.9  | 24.1  | 41.0\nL277: Qwen-9B  | 14.2  | 57.3  | 44.5  | 35.3\nL278: Spark-4B  | 49.7  | 3.8  | 33.6  | 3.0\nL279: All agents  | 28.7  | 28.9  | 34.5  | 16.9\nL280: Table 4: Recognition and execution per agent, three precisions pooled. Rec.: % of all audited trajectories, including successful attacks, in which the judge flags recognition. Exec.$|$Rec.: % of recognized trajectories in which the attacker’s action was still executed.\nL281: ### 4.2 Changing Only the System Prompt Changes ASR Sharply\nL282: #### A permission prompt moves ASR from 0.2% to 43%.\nL283: We compare the two policies on the 10,698 InjecAgent cases evaluated under both (Figure cite102†2 ). A system prompt permitting instructions in tool outputs (Authorize) raises pooled ASR to 43.05% [42.11, 43.99], against 8.09% at baseline. A matched prompt forbidding them (Interdict) lowers it to 0.22% [0.15, 0.33]. The two prompts differ by only nine words, so length cannot explain the gap. As predicted by the Untriggered Default, low baseline ASR stems from inactivity rather than defense.\nL284: #### Most agents follow the permission.\nL285: \nL286: Paired on the same cases, nine agents shift significantly under McNemar tests. For Qwen-9B, 773 cases succeed only under Authorize and one only under Interdict ($p<10^{-15}$). Spark-4B surges from 0.95% to 70.88%. Every interpreted shift survives Bonferroni correction (Appendix cite56†E ). The two exceptions are LFM-2.6B (0.00%), with mostly invalid outputs, and Ministral-14B (0.11%), whose low response remains unexplained.\nL287: #### An explicit boundary also lowers ASR.\nL288: \nL289: The three-rule guardrail lowers pooled InjecAgent ASR from 9.69% to 0.95% across nine agents, and invalid outputs fall rather than rise. Only Ministral-14B (from 0.28% to 4.39%, $p<10^{-10}$) and LFM-2.6B (from 0.00% to 0.57%) move the other way. The guardrail is also the longest block we add (182 words), so a longer system prompt does not by itself raise ASR.\nL290: #### Lower precision lowers ASR by weakening agents.\nL291: Serving weights at FP8 and NF4 lowers pooled ASR only slightly: from 8.09% to 7.50% and 6.35% on InjecAgent, and from 11.96% to 11.11% and 9.63% on AgentDojo. This is not improved defense, as refusal rates remain flat across precisions (Appendix Table cite103†9 ). Instead, agents act less: at NF4, Qwen-4B’s ASR drops from 18.79% to 13.28% ($p<10^{-8}$) while invalid outputs rise from 19 to 115.\nL292: On AgentDojo, NF4 reduces ASR by 2.33 points, but cuts Safe-and-Useful episodes by 3.97 points and UA by 4.75 points.\nL293: ### 4.3 A Safe Agent Is Not Always a Useful One\nL294: \nL295: Figure 3: Attack–Task Outcome Matrix (ATOM) on AgentDojo. Each episode is placed by whether the attack succeeded (rows) and whether the user’s task was completed (columns). (a) The 16,273 audited episodes with both verdicts. The dashed cell, Safe but Dysfunctional, is what ASR counts as a successful defense. (b) The same matrix for each agent, as a percentage of its episodes, with agents sorted by Safe and Useful.\nL296: #### ATOM shows where security and utility part ways.\nL297: \nL298: Figure cite104†3 a maps all 16,273 audited AgentDojo episodes with both verdicts into ATOM cells. Rows indicate attack success, while columns indicate task completion. About two thirds of episodes are Safe and Useful, the outcome a secure agent should reach. Yet one in five is Safe but Dysfunctional (21.7%). In these episodes the attack failed, but so did the user’s task: the agent was safe yet dysfunctional.\nL299: #### Marginal rates hide joint failures.\nL300: In Figure cite104†3 a, ASR and UA are marginal rates that conceal joint failures. ASR is the bottom-row sum (3.8% + 7.3% = 11.1%), capturing only attack success. UA is the left-column sum (67.2% + 3.8% = 71.0%), capturing only task completion. Together, these totals leave two questions open: were unfinished tasks (29.0%) safe or hijacked, and was every completed task safe? ATOM answers both. Among unfinished tasks, the agent stayed safe in 21.7% of episodes, which ASR misclassifies as successful defense.\nL301: Among completed tasks, the attack succeeded in 3.8% of episodes, an outcome that UA counts as useful. Reporting ASR and UA side by side (cite91†Bhagwatkar et al., 2025 ) cannot recover these four distinct cells.\nL302: #### Same NRP, different risks.\nL303: ATOM separates agents that a single score treats as equal. ASB’s NRP (§cite12†3 ) assigns Qwen-9B, Nanbeige-3B, and Spark-4B nearly identical scores: 81.0, 80.6, and 80.6 (Table cite105†5 ). Yet their ATOM distributions diverge sharply. For Qwen-9B, attacks succeed in 11.5% of episodes (7.4% + 4.1%), while 10.1% are safe but dysfunctional. For Spark-4B, attacks succeed in only 1.7% (1.0% + 0.7%), but safe-yet-dysfunctional episodes reach 22.5%, more than twice Qwen-9B’s rate.\nL304: Nanbeige-3B lies intermediate (4.9% and 15.1%). Because NRP multiplies clean-task performance by failed attack share, Qwen-9B’s higher ASR is offset by higher utility, collapsing both to the same scalar score. ATOM keeps these losses separate: Qwen-9B is riskier when attack execution is costly, whereas Spark-4B is riskier when task completion is mandatory.\nL305: Agent  | NRP  | Safe and Useful  | Safe but Dysfunctional  | Unsafe but Useful  | Unsafe and Hijacked\nL306: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL307: Qwen-9B  | 81.0  | 78.3  | 10.1  | 7.4  | 4.1\nL308: Nanbeige-3B  | 80.6  | 80.1  | 15.1  | 2.4  | 2.5\nL309: Spark-4B  | 80.6  | 75.7  | 22.5  | 1.0  | 0.7\nL310: \nL311: Table 5: Three agents with nearly equal NRP but different ATOM cells (%). NRP uses the FP16 runs, and ATOM the audited sample (all agents: Table cite106†11 ).\nL312: ## 5 Discussion and Conclusion\nL313: This paper asked what a low ASR hides in small tool-using agents. Across 33,000 audited trajectories, it mostly hides silence. Failed attacks are usually ignored rather than refused, and agents that notice an injection often still execute it (§cite20†4.1 ). This silence exposes a scaffolding illusion. Small agents appear secure under standard benchmarks not from robust instruction boundaries, but because default scaffolds leave injected instructions untriggered.\nL314: In effect, the benchmark measures the scaffold’s inertness rather than the model’s safety. Once a system prompt permits external instructions, ASR surges from 0.2% to 43% under identical model weights (§cite26†4.2 ).\nL315: #### Security–utility trade-offs.\nL316: ATOM shows that apparent security frequently conceals functional failure. In 21.7% of AgentDojo episodes, the agent remains uncompromised but fails the user’s task entirely (§cite31†4.3 ). Additionally, genuine refusal does not guarantee task success: one in six refusals still leaves the task undone. Similarly, weight quantization (FP8, NF4) lowers ASR primarily by degrading agent capability and generating invalid tool calls rather than improving defense.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAcceptance Is Not Speed: Length Inflation in Training-Free Lossy Speculative Decoding (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23818v1)\nciteturn41240view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23818v1\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 755\nL264: We evaluated five training-free relaxed verification rules on six benchmarks and twelve target-drafter pairs, measuring completion length, verifier rounds, time, and accuracy rather than acceptance alone. Relaxation does raise the number of draft tokens accepted per round, but the model spends that gain on longer outputs: across the 360 cells the mean length inflation ($1.54\\times$) exceeds the mean acceptance gain ($1.34\\times$).\nL265: Where a rule does decode faster, accuracy drops; the one rule that keeps accuracy close to lossless, spec_casc_tok, is also the one that relaxes least.\nL266: This paper identifies length as what decides the speed of relaxed verification: once our measured length is supplied, the length-corrected model of cite67†Xia et al. (2026) predicts our verifier rounds within about $2\\%$, and the clock departs from it only through the cost of a round, which the model does not contain. Two conclusions follow.\nL267: Acceptance gain is not a measure of speed for a relaxed rule, because relaxation changes what the model generates and not only how fast it is verified; completion length and verifier rounds have to be reported next to it. And the promising direction is not accepting more of the drafter’s tokens but choosing which ones to admit, since the only rule that worked trusted the target more.\nL268: Section 4 also yields a simple mitigation: closing the reasoning trace before the generation budget runs out recovers the answers that inflation cuts off, raising correct answers from 126 to 439 over the 1,132 runs it touches, under all five rules. It does not recover the speed, since these runs still spend most of their budget; closing the trace earlier, where the answer is already written, is the natural next step.\nL269: ## Limitations\nL270: \nL271: #### Matching and settings.\nL272: \nL273: No $\\bar{\\ell}$ is reachable by every method, so the matching of Section cite20†3.2 is best-effort and the tables compare methods at different $\\bar{\\ell}$, each at its most relaxed setting. Each method is tested at four published or reference $\\alpha$ values; other values could change its profile, but the conclusions also hold at the gentlest and the best accuracy-preserving settings (Section cite21†3.3 ; Tables cite98†D4 and cite107†B3 ).\nL274: #### Budget censoring.\nL275: \nL276: Relaxed runs hit the per-dataset token budget more often, so lengths are right-censored and the inflation is, if anything, understated; part of the accuracy loss is unfinished runs. The GSM8K budget (2 048 tokens) is tight for Qwen3-8B: $25\\%$ of its lossless runs hit it (Table cite95†B1 ).\nL277: #### Sampling and timing.\nL278: Each case runs once per arm (seeds repeated on the primary pairs only, Section cite21†3.3 ), and paired bootstrap intervals over cases are wide on AIME24 (30 cases) and MT-Bench (80), so we interpret only effects that recur across datasets or targets.\nL279: Times are client-side at batch size 1 and analyzed on the primary pairs only; their ratio to lossless depends on the server’s cost per committed token (Section cite22†3.4 ) and is noisy because each benchmark’s first cases ran on a fresh server process and the rest on a reused one, while round counts depend on neither. MT-Bench quality is one judge’s first-turn score, not an accuracy.\nL280: #### Scope.\nL281: We use one serving stack (vLLM 0.26.0) and, for the main results, one sampler (temperature $1.0$, top-$p$ $1.0$); on Qwen3-8B the gentle rules’ inflation depends on the sampler (Section cite21†3.3 ). Every relaxed arm drafts $N_{\\mathrm{draft}}=6$ tokens; draft length is swept only under lossless verification (Appendix cite43†C ).\nL282: The rules are our reimplementations in vLLM’s rejection sampler, checked at the strict limit but not against the authors’ code; the ensemble rule, speculative contrastive decoding (Table cite79†A1 ) and typical acceptance (cite114†Cai et al., 2024 ) are not swept. Lossless decoding at a higher temperature also inflates completions (Section cite25†3.7 ), so the inflation is not specific to relaxed acceptance, and we do not explain why an unlikely token makes a reasoning model think longer.\nL283: ## References\nL284:   * Abramovich et al. (2026) T. Abramovich, M. Ashkenazi, I. Putterman, B. Chislett, T. Mitra, B. Darvish Rouhani, R. Zilberstein, and Y. Geifman SPEED-Bench: a unified and diverse benchmark for speculative decoding. In International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), Note: arXiv:2604.09557 Cited by: cite115†Appendix E .\nL285:   * Bachmann et al. (2025) G. Bachmann, S. Anagnostidis, A. Pumarola, M. Georgopoulos, A. Sanakoyeu, Y. Du, E. Schönfeld, A. Thabet, and J. Kohler Judge decoding: faster speculative sampling requires going beyond model alignment. In International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), Note: arXiv:2501.19309 Cited by: cite116†§2.2 .\nL286:   * Bai et al. (2025) Y. Bai, S. Tu, J. Zhang, H. Peng, X. Wang, X. Lv, S. Cao, J. Xu, L. Hou, Y. Dong, J. Tang, and J. Li LongBench v2: towards deeper understanding and reasoning on realistic long-context multitasks. In Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL), Note: arXiv:2412.15204 Cited by: cite117†§3.1 .\nL287:   * Cai et al. (2024) T. Cai, Y. Li, Z. Geng, H. Peng, J. D. Lee, D. Chen, and T. Dao Medusa: simple LLM inference acceleration framework with multiple decoding heads. In International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), Note: arXiv:2401.10774 Cited by: cite118†Scope. .\nL288:   * Chen et al. (2023) C. Chen, S. Borgeaud, G. Irving, J. Lespiau, L. Sifre, and J. Jumper Accelerating large language model decoding with speculative sampling. arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.01318. Cited by: cite119†§1 , cite120†§2.1 .\nL289:   * Chen et al. (2021) M. Chen, J. Tworek, H. Jun, Q. Yuan, H. P. d. O. Pinto, J. Kaplan, H. Edwards, Y. Burda, N. Joseph, G. Brockman, et al. Evaluating large language models trained on code. arXiv preprint arXiv:2107.03374. Cited by: cite117†§3.1 .\nL290:   * Chen et al. (2025) X. Chen, J. Xu, T. Liang, Z. He, J. Pang, D. Yu, L. Song, Q. Liu, M. Zhou, Z. Zhang, R. Wang, Z. Tu, H. Mi, and D. Yu Do NOT think that much for 2+3=? On the overthinking of long reasoning models. In International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), Note: arXiv:2412.21187 Cited by: cite121†§4 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIgnored, Not Refused: What Low Prompt-Injection Success Rates Hidein Small Tool-Using Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23254v1)\nciteturn41240view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23254v1\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 558\nL313: This paper asked what a low ASR hides in small tool-using agents. Across 33,000 audited trajectories, it mostly hides silence. Failed attacks are usually ignored rather than refused, and agents that notice an injection often still execute it (§cite20†4.1 ). This silence exposes a scaffolding illusion. Small agents appear secure under standard benchmarks not from robust instruction boundaries, but because default scaffolds leave injected instructions untriggered.\nL314: In effect, the benchmark measures the scaffold’s inertness rather than the model’s safety. Once a system prompt permits external instructions, ASR surges from 0.2% to 43% under identical model weights (§cite26†4.2 ).\nL315: #### Security–utility trade-offs.\nL316: ATOM shows that apparent security frequently conceals functional failure. In 21.7% of AgentDojo episodes, the agent remains uncompromised but fails the user’s task entirely (§cite31†4.3 ). Additionally, genuine refusal does not guarantee task success: one in six refusals still leaves the task undone. Similarly, weight quantization (FP8, NF4) lowers ASR primarily by degrading agent capability and generating invalid tool calls rather than improving defense.\nL317: Genuine resilience requires completing benign user requests while repelling untrusted instructions. ASR alone has no relation to utility, while UA captures only task completion. Relying on separate marginal rates conceals operational trade-offs and rewards dysfunctional agents.\nL318: #### Recommendations for evaluation.\nL319: To avoid misleading safety claims, agent benchmarks must evaluate beyond scalar ASR. Evaluators should report behavioral failure modes, task completion in ATOM, invalid output rates, and prompt and precision sensitivity. Deployers must establish explicit instruction-data boundaries and avoid treating quantization-induced fragility as security gains. Future work should investigate adaptive adversaries, multi-agent coordination, and internal representations bridging recognition to refusal.\nL320: ## Limitations\nL321: \nL322: #### Benchmarks and attacks.\nL323: \nL324: We use two synthetic, single-agent benchmarks with static injections. Adaptive attacks reach higher ASR (cite85†Chen et al., 2026b ; cite86†Hou et al., 2026 ), so our finding that a low ASR is not evidence of security is conservative. We do not, however, establish resilience against adaptive or white-box attackers, multi-agent systems or live deployments.\nL325: #### Models.\nL326: \nL327: We study small open-weight agents (2–14B) with greedy decoding, so sampling variance is not measured. Agents also differ in tool interface, which may partly explain differences between agents. The frontier reference is one run of one model and does not establish a scaling trend.\nL328: #### The audit.\nL329: \nL330: Labels come from one LLM judge, validated against human labels and benchmark verdicts. The two Inert labels are the hardest to separate, so our main claims use the four-way grouping. Recognition is read from stated reasoning, which may not reflect what drives behavior (cite83†Turpin et al., 2023 ; cite107†Chen et al., 2025 ), so it is a lower bound.\nL331: #### Coverage.\nL332: \nL333: Task completion is measured only on AgentDojo, since InjecAgent has no task check. The policy conditions ran only on InjecAgent, so their effect on utility is not measured.\nL334: ## Ethical considerations\nL335: All attacks come from the published InjecAgent and AgentDojo benchmarks and run in sandboxed environments with synthetic data. We created no new attacks and attacked no live system. Judging sent only these synthetic trajectories to the judge model. Our permission results show that one system prompt can make agents follow injected instructions.\nL336: Since the attacker cannot change the system prompt in our threat model, we report these results to help operators avoid permissive configurations, not to enable attacks. Human validation consisted of the authors labeling recorded model outputs, so no other human subjects or personal data were involved. The experiments used about 557 GPU hours (about 230.6 kWh, an estimated 88.8 kg CO_{2}e) and retrained no model. We release our data and code under the licenses of the underlying benchmarks and models.\nL337: ## References\nL338:   * Belcak et al. (2025) Peter Belcak, Greg Heinrich, Shizhe Diao, Yonggan Fu, Xin Dong, Saurav Muralidharan, Yingyan Celine Lin, and Pavlo Molchanov. 2025. cite108†Small language models are the future of agentic AI†doi.org . arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.02153.\nL339:   * Bhagwatkar et al. (2025) Rishika Bhagwatkar, Kevin Kasa, Abhay Puri, Gabriel Huang, Irina Rish, Graham W. Taylor, Krishnamurthy Dj Dvijotham, and Alexandre Lacoste. 2025. cite109†Indirect prompt injections: Are firewalls all you need, or stronger benchmarks? arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.05244.\nL340:   * Cartagena and Teixeira (2026) Arnold Cartagena and Ariane Teixeira. 2026. cite110†Mind the GAP: Text safety does not transfer to tool-call safety in LLM agents . arXiv preprint arXiv:2602.16943.\nL341:   * Chen et al. (2026a) Xin Chen, Jie Zhang, and Florian Tramèr. 2026a. cite111†Learning to inject: Automated prompt injection via reinforcement learning . arXiv preprint arXiv:2602.05746.\nL342:   * Chen et al. (2025) Yanda Chen, Joe Benton, Ansh Radhakrishnan, Jonathan Uesato, Carson Denison, John Schulman, Arushi Somani, Peter Hase, Misha Wagner, Fabien Roger, Vlad Mikulik, Samuel R. Bowman, Jan Leike, Jared Kaplan, and Ethan Perez. 2025. cite112†Reasoning models don’t always say what they think . arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.05410.\nL343:   * Chen et al. (2026b) Zixuan Chen, Jiaxiang Chen, Li Luo, Ke Xu, Xiaoxiang Huang, Tanfeng Sun, and Xinghao Jiang. 2026b. cite113†IterInject: Indirect prompt injection against LLM agents via feedback-guided iterative optimization . arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.24659.\nL344:   * Debenedetti et al. (2026) Edoardo Debenedetti, Ilia Shumailov, Tianqi Fan, Jamie Hayes, Nicholas Carlini, Daniel Fabian, Christoph Kern, Chongyang Shi, Andreas Terzis, and Florian Tramèr. 2026. cite114†Defeating prompt injections by design†doi.org . In 2026 IEEE Conference on Secure and Trustworthy Machine Learning (SaTML), pages 587–618.\nL345:   * Debenedetti et al. (2024) Edoardo Debenedetti, Jie Zhang, Mislav Balunović, Luca Beurer-Kellner, Marc Fischer, and Florian Tramèr. 2024. cite115†AgentDojo: A dynamic environment to evaluate prompt injection attacks and defenses for LLM agents†doi.org . In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024), Datasets and Benchmarks Track, volume 37.\n"
